\subsection{Where regression wins and where it loses}

The regression has smaller RMSE than DM whenever the spillover share is large enough for DM's bias $-s\gate$ to exceed the regression's error (Figure~\ref{fig:regime}; ``wins'' is the paired interval below zero). Over the grid of this study (Table~\ref{tab:regime}, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}) regression wins in 79.6\%, 77.8\%, 69.9\%, 55.8\% and 31.3\% of cells at $\sigma=0.25,0.5,1,2,4$ ($\sigma=2,4$ are post-freeze extensions); the shares describe this grid, not a population of networks. At $\sigma=0.5$ it is 0.49 for the threshold mechanism (d) and 0.75--0.87 for the others. The break-even spillover share $s^*$ at $d=20$ is about 0.19 for (a), (b), (c), (e), 0.25 for (f), and does not exist for (d), where regression loses at every share on every synthetic graph; at $d=80$ it is 0.38 for (a) and 0.54 for (d); at $d=5$ regression wins at every share on the grid, so only $s^*\le0.1$ is available.

The failure on the threshold mechanism does not transfer to the thinned real graphs: regression wins on (d) at all three degrees (log ratio $-1.19$ to $-0.33$ on ego-Facebook, $-1.15$ to $-0.89$ on Stanford3) and in every other real-graph row except (b) at $d=80$ on ego-Facebook, where it wins in two of three shares. The real graphs have higher clustering and a different degree tail, so we do not say which property matters.
